<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bevin–Sforza plan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bevin-Sforza plan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bevin–Sforza plan was a proposal agreed in May 1949 by the British foreign secretary Ernest Bevin and the Italian foreign minister Carlo Sforza for the future of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s former colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. It would have placed Tripolitania under Italian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> under British and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> under French trusteeship for ten years. Opposed in Libya, it was rejected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> by a single vote.</p></div>
